Event delegation attaches a single listener to a common ancestor and uses `event.target` to handle events from many children, leveraging event bubbling. It saves memory (one listener instead of hundreds) and automatically handles elements added to the DOM later.
```js
list.addEventListener("click", e => {
  const li = e.target.closest("li");
  if (li) select(li.dataset.id);
});
```

A closure is a function that keeps access to variables from its lexical scope even after the outer function has returned. It's the basis for data privacy, factories and memoization.
```js
function counter(){ let n = 0; return () => ++n; }
const next = counter();
next(); next(); // 2
```

Use a hash map of value → index. For each number check whether `target - n` was already seen. **O(n)** time, **O(n)** space — better than the O(n²) brute force.
```js
function twoSum(nums, t){
  const seen = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++){
    if (seen.has(t - nums[i])) return [seen.get(t - nums[i]), i];
    seen.set(nums[i], i);
  }
}
```

Walk the list re-pointing each node's `next` to the previous node, tracking prev/curr/next. **O(n)** time, **O(1)** space.
```js
function reverse(head){
  let prev = null;
  while (head){
    const next = head.next;
    head.next = prev;
    prev = head;
    head = next;
  }
  return prev;
}
```

`satisfies` checks that a value conforms to a type **without widening** its inferred type, so you keep the precise literal types while still validating the shape. Added in TS 4.9.
```ts
const routes = {
  home: "/", user: "/u/:id"
} satisfies Record<string, string>;
// routes.home is "/", not just string
```
